    "notes": [
      "Of the nasal vowels, only ä is written. Ë, ï and ü exist in the language, but they are usually not marked.",
      "The glottal stop comes after ch, k, t and ts (k'eñä), and before b, m, n, ñ, r, u and y ('ba, 'ñu).",
      "Tones are not written in words. In the verb proclitics, the high tone is marked with an accent because it changes the meaning: dí xipi «I tell him», di xipi «he tells him».",
      "Pronunciation is shown in brackets with the International Phonetic Alphabet (IPA). U sounds [w] before another vowel: ua «foot». X sounds like «sh» [ʃ], and j like the Spanish j [x]."
    ]
  },
  "grammar": {
    "intro": "To conjugate a verb in Hñähñu, put a proclitic before the root. The proclitic says who does the action and when.",
    "blocks": [
      {
        "items": [
          {
            "slot": "Proclitic",
            "description": "Person and tense. With hin- in front, the sentence is negative."
          },
          {
            "slot": "Root",
            "description": "The verb. In the preterite and the future its first letter can change."
          },
          {
            "slot": "Plural",
            "description": "Suffix -hu̱ for «we» and «you all»."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "The tone of the proclitic changes the meaning",
        "description": "Look at the accent: it marks a high tone.",
        "items": [
          {
            "translation": "I tell him/her"
          },
          {
            "translation": "he/she tells him/her"
          },
          {
            "translation": "I told him/her"
          },
          {
            "translation": "he/she will tell him/her"
          },
          {
            "translation": "you told him/her"
          },
          {
            "translation": "I will tell him/her"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "The root changes too",
        "description": "In the preterite and the future, many roots change their first consonant. That is how the secondary forms of the root are made.",
        "items": [
          {
            "name": "Form B",
            "description": "The consonant becomes voiced: p → b, t → d, th → d, ts → z, k → g, j → g, f → m.",
            "examples": [
              {
                "meaning": "he/she knew it"
              },
              {
                "meaning": "he/she bought it"
              },
              {
                "meaning": "he/she ground it"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "Form Y",
            "description": "If the root starts with a vowel or with h + vowel, y is added (or ñ if the vowel is nasal).",
            "examples": [
              {
                "meaning": "he/she looked for it"
              },
              {
                "meaning": "he/she heard it"
              },
              {
                "meaning": "he/she slept"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "Form N",
            "description": "Some verbs keep their consonant but add n- (or m-). The dictionary shows it with the 1st person of the preterite.",
            "examples": [
              {
                "meaning": "I jumped"
              },
              {
                "meaning": "he/she laughed"
              },
              {
                "meaning": "I drank water"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "blocks": [
          {
            "title": "The plural with -hu̱",
            "description": "In everyday speech, the suffix -hu̱ changes the end of the root:",
            "examples": [
              {
                "meaning": "drink"
              },
              {
                "meaning": "work"
              },
              {
                "meaning": "look"
              },
              {
                "meaning": "think"
              },
              {
                "meaning": "buy"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "title": "Being something: proclitic + ra",
            "description": "To say «I am», «you are» or «he/she is» with a noun, the proclitic joins the article ra.",
            "columns": [
